Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Five Reeds Hold While the Floor Moves
- Story & Context: Lin begins with a five-note Dorian sheng field and refuses to change the full chord for the first ninety seconds. Tomás cycles three different batida accent maps beneath it: first the kick leans forward, then the clap becomes the dominant marker, then both retreat while low toms imply a new center. Rui replaces every fourth electronic kick with one damped bass-marimba note. Qiao enters only when one upper sheng pipe is removed, using a narrow viola scrape to expose the missing pitch as negative space. The track demonstrates the genre’s central idea: the harmony stays nearly still while the rhythmic floor keeps turning.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Weather-chart paper covered with three different red accent grids beneath one unchanged five-note staff, brass sheng pipes casting long shadows across a concrete table, bass-marimba mallet and prepared viola bridge in frame. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “five reeds hold,” “move the floor,” “same air,” and “remove one voice.”

Track 2: Marimba Replaces Kick Seventeen
- Story & Context: The second track starts with the driest beat on the EP, a clipped 130 BPM pattern built from kick, clap, rim, and two toms. On the seventeenth kick event of each long cycle, Tomás mutes the machine and Rui inserts a low marimba dead stroke instead. At first the substitution is obvious; later, Rui changes mallet hardness and damping until wood and electronics become difficult to distinguish. Lin gradually adds upper reed tones without changing the root field, while Qiao uses muted tremolo in the spaces where the kick has been replaced. The last cycle removes the electronic kick entirely and lets marimba, hand percussion, and sheng carry the groove.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close view of a numbered weather-log sheet with “17” circled in red, one dark bass-marimba bar beside a compact drum machine with a single muted pad, sheng bell chamber and viola string preparations in shallow focus. Spoken fragments limited to “seventeen is wood,” “mute the machine,” “same pulse,” and “let the bar answer.”

Track 3: Pressure Map Ends Without a Chord Change
- Story & Context: The closer begins with the widest sheng voicing on the record, spread across low and high pipes while Tomás uses a sparse half-empty batida pattern. Instead of building toward a harmonic resolution, the ensemble subtracts. Rui drops to isolated low octaves. Qiao removes her preparations one by one, making the viola gradually more resonant. Tomás reduces the beat until only one clap and one low kick remain. Lin keeps the same chord family throughout but changes which internal reed is audible with each breath direction. The final minute contains only sheng and room tone. The piece ends when Lin closes one pipe and stops breathing; there is no cadence.
